          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "The risk check returned a routine tier, not an emergency or urgent one. It also said this is not a clearance. The tool only checks a fixed list of warning patterns, so it cannot rule out a serious cause. Your symptoms have lasted for months and include a racing heart on exertion and light-headedness, so please arrange a medical assessment soon rather than waiting. If you become faint, have chest pain or pressure, struggle to breathe, or pass out, seek emergency care at once.\n\n**What iron deficiency can do to the blood:** The guidance says iron-deficiency anemia often causes fatigue, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations (a racing or pounding heartbeat), dizziness, headache and pale skin. Onset is usually gradual, and the body compensates well, so people often blame months of tiredness on other things until the body can no longer keep up (Iron-deficiency anemia, General clinical reference). The same reference notes that ferritin is the main test of iron stores, but it can look normal during inflammation, so other iron tests may be needed.\n\n**Heavy periods:** The retrieved material does not connect heavy periods to iron deficiency directly, so I cannot tell you from these sources whether they are the cause. Heavy menstrual bleeding is one common reason for low iron, but that is a general point outside what I retrieved. A clinician can check the blood count and iron studies.\n\n**ICD-10 chapter for anemia:** Anemia is in Chapter 3 of ICD-10-CM, \"blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism,\" codes D50 to D89. Iron-deficiency anemia is at D50, the first block of nutritional anemias (ICD-10-CM Chapter 3). Chapter 9 (I00 to I99) covers the heart and circulation, which is a separate chapter.\n\n**What the sources did not cover:** I could not find guidance on the cause of your symptoms, on what tests suit your situation, or on treatment. Please raise these questions with a clinician, who can take your full history into account.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
